/**

\page design How Fl_Dock works

Fl_Dock lets the user arrange an application's panels: side by side, stacked as tabs, or floating in their own
windows, by dragging them, and lets the application save and restore the arrangement.  It is an extension of FLTK
1.4 and later, written to FLTK's conventions.

# Goals {#design_goals}

- Work wherever FLTK works: Windows, macOS, X11 and Wayland.  Nothing may depend on placing top-level windows,
  global mouse positions, translucent windows or a compositor.  Where the platform gives them, screen positions
  are used as an enhancement: dragging between top-level windows and placing a floating window by the mouse.
- Be an FLTK library: FLTK's names, style and license, no other dependency, no STL in public headers, no
  exceptions or RTTI, documented with Doxygen.
- Make the common case short: an application adds its panels, gives a default layout in a few calls, and gets
  dragging, tabs, floating windows and saved layouts.
- Hold any widget as a panel, including an `Fl_Gl_Window`.

# The model: the widget tree {#design_model}

A docking layout is a tree whose inner nodes are splits and whose leaves are tab groups.  Fl_Dock makes the FLTK
widget tree itself that tree, as Fl_Tile, Fl_Tabs and Fl_Flex do: the groups are the nodes, so there is nothing to
keep in step and an application can walk or inspect the layout with ordinary FLTK calls.  A saved layout is parsed
into a small description first, and widgets are touched only once it is known to be good, so restoring is
all-or-nothing.

| Class | Is | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| `Fl_Dock` | the dock: an `Fl_Group` | one node, the root of its tree |
| `Fl_Dock_Split` | an inner node | two or more nodes side by side or one above another, each with a weight |
| `Fl_Dock_Tabs` | a leaf | one or more panels as tabs, one shown |
| `Fl_Dock_Panel` | a dockable panel: an `Fl_Group` | the application's widgets, and a name and a title |
| `Fl_Dock_Window` | a floating, inset or sliding window | an `Fl_Dock` of its own |

A dock may also have a center: one widget, such as a 3D view, that is a leaf of its own, is never closed or
floated, and keeps the space a dock gives up when its panels close.  Panels can be docked beside it.

After every change the tree is normalized: an empty tab group is removed, a split with one child is
replaced by that child, and a split inside a parent of the same orientation is merged into it.  The weights are
carried along so the sizes do not jump.

# Panels {#design_panels}

A panel's label is its title, shown on its tab.  Its name, which must be unique and stable because layouts are
saved by name, is the label unless `name()` sets it; the dock keeps the label as the name when it is given the
panel, so a title changed later does not change the name.  A dock refuses a panel without a name or with one it
already has.  Flags say whether a panel can be closed, floated, or moved at all.  Closing a panel hides it and
removes it from the tree but keeps it, together with where it was, so showing it again puts it back.  The dock never
deletes a panel while the panel belongs to it, so its state survives every move; it deletes its panels, closed ones
included, with itself, as any FLTK group deletes its children.

# Lifetime and order {#design_lifetime}

- `remove_panel()` takes a panel out of the dock for good, out of its stripes, menus and records too, and gives it
  back to the application.  Deleting a panel does the same first, so `delete panel` is safe, as for any FLTK child.
  Deleting a floating window deletes the panels in it, which leave the dock the same way.
- `center(w)` replaces the center: the new one takes the old one's place, and the old one is hidden and given back
  to the application.  `center(0)` takes it out.  Deleting the center takes it out of the tree.
- A layout may be restored before the application's window is shown, the usual order: floating windows are shown
  with the dock's window and hidden with it, so closing the application's window closes them and `Fl::run()`
  returns.
- Closed panels keep their own size while the dock is resized.

# Programmatic layout {#design_layout}

```cpp
Fl_Dock *dock = new Fl_Dock(0, 25, 800, 575);
dock->center(view);                                     // the 3D view
dock->add_panel(files, FL_DOCK_LEFT, 0, 0.25);          // left of everything, a quarter of the width
dock->add_panel(props, FL_DOCK_BOTTOM, files, 0.5);     // below the file browser
dock->add_panel(log, FL_DOCK_TAB, props);               // a tab beside the properties
```

Unlike other groups, a dock does not become the current group when it is made: panels are given to it with
`add_panel()`, and may be made anywhere.

`add_panel(panel, where, ref, fraction)` places a panel on the given side of another panel, of the center, or of
the whole dock when `ref` is 0, taking that fraction of the space, or as a tab of `ref`; a panel already in the dock
is moved.  `float_panel()` floats it, `dock_panel()` docks a floating panel where it was, `close_panel()` closes it
and `show_panel()` brings it back or forward.  The names end in `_panel` because `add()` and `show()` already mean
something to every FLTK group.

As the dock is resized, docked panels keep their sizes and the part of the dock holding the center takes the
change, as in most applications with docking; panels are made smaller in proportion only once the center would
have less than a third of the room.  `keep_sizes(0)` makes every part keep its share instead.

`place_panel()` is `add_panel()` for a panel created after a layout was restored: if the layout had a panel of that
name, it goes where the layout put it, and otherwise where the arguments say.  An application that creates its
panels lazily uses it so they open where the user left them.

Panels are shown and hidden only while they are inside a window.  A panel may hold a subwindow, such as an
`Fl_Gl_Window`, and a subwindow cannot be mapped, or on macOS even resized, outside a window; a tab group built
outside one only records which tab it shows, and the dock shows that tab once the group is in place.  For the same
reason the dock only marks a node visible until its window is shown: `show()` on an `Fl_Gl_Window` whose window is
not shown yet leaves it drawing nowhere on macOS.

# Dragging {#design_dragging}

A drag starts on a tab, which moves its panel, or on the empty part of a tab strip, which moves the whole group.
While it lasts, the drop target is found, and the place the panel would take is shown:

1. over a tab strip: as a tab at that position;
2. within a band along the dock's own edge: beside the whole dock;
3. over a tab group or the center, in the outer third on any side: beside it on that side;
4. otherwise, in a tab group's middle: as a tab of it.

The tab strip comes first, because a strip along the top of the dock lies inside the edge band and could otherwise
never be dropped on.

The indicator is a frame drawn by child windows of the target's top-level window, which stack above an OpenGL
subwindow on every platform and need no compositor.  Esc cancels a drag, and a drag that ends where it began
changes nothing.

A drag is ordinary FL_PUSH, FL_DRAG and FL_RELEASE events, which go to the window the drag started in for as long
as the button is down.  Within that window they are in its coordinates.  To find a target in another of the dock's
windows, the mouse's screen position is compared with that window's; Windows, macOS and X11 give both.  Wayland
gives neither, so there a drag only finds targets in its own window, and a panel dropped outside it floats in a new
window whose place is the compositor's choice.  Moving a panel into another window is always possible from the tab
strip's menu, which floats a panel or docks it back, so no arrangement depends on dragging between windows.

FLTK 1.5's Fl_Dockable_Group drags groups between windows on every platform, Wayland included, with
Wayland's `xdg_toplevel_drag`.  Fl_Dock does not use it, so that it works with FLTK 1.4.

# Floating windows {#design_floating}

A floating window holds a whole dock, so panels can be docked into it as into the main one.  It closes when its last
panel leaves.

An inset window is a floating window kept inside the dock's own window: a subwindow over the dock with a frame drawn by
Fl_Dock, which the user moves within the dock's area and resizes from any edge or corner.  It has no title bar of its
own; its tab strips serve as one, so the panel's title, close button and menu appear once.  The part of a strip beside
the tabs moves the window, and some of it is always kept free of tabs for that.  A sliding window has no title bar
either; its tab menu offers Pin.  Because it is a subwindow it moves, hides and minimizes with the main window on every
platform, Wayland included, and needs no window manager.  `inset_panel()` makes one; the tab strip's menu offers Float,
Float Inside and Dock, whichever do not describe where the panel already is.  The dock keeps its inset windows inside
its area as it is resized.  A layout saves them as `inset x y w h`, relative to the dock.

A panel has a mode, docked, inset, floating or sliding, and remembers the inset and floating windows it was last in.
A closed panel keeps its mode, so `show_panel()` opens it the way it was shown before, in a window where it was.  An
application can give the dock a closed panel with `store_panel()` and set its mode to open it inset at first.  A
layout saves the mode of closed panels that open in a window as `opens name inset x y w h`.  Every floating
operation is also available without dragging, from a panel's menu, because dragging between windows is the part most
at the mercy of the platform.

A floating window is placed where it is asked, or by the mouse, and each time it is shown it is moved and made
smaller to fit the work area of the screen nearest its middle, so a layout saved with a screen that is gone opens on
one that is there.  While dragging, floating windows are looked at before the main dock, as they stay above it.

Panels may share a place group, set with `place_group()`: editors for different kinds of item that take turns in
one place.  Showing one closes the one of its group that is shown and opens it where the last of the group was,
docked in the same place with the same share or in an inset or floating window at the same place and size, so the
place belongs to the group rather than to each panel.

# Stripes and sliding panels {#design_stripes}

A dock may show a stripe of buttons on any of its sides, chosen with `stripes()`; a stripe with no buttons takes no
space unless asked to.  `stripe_panel()` gives a panel a button on a side, in one of two groups, at the start and at
the end of the stripe.  The button shows the panel's icon, or its title turned along the stripe, and is drawn pressed
while the panel is shown.

Clicking a button, or pressing the panel's `dock_shortcut()`, shows a hidden panel where it was and gives it the
keyboard, and hides a shown one.  With `FL_DOCK_STRIPE_FOCUS_FIRST` a click on a shown panel without the keyboard
gives it the keyboard instead.  Showing a panel hides the others of its stripe group, unless `FL_DOCK_STRIPE_SHARED`
is set, so a group behaves as tabs whose tabs are on the stripe.  A button's menu pins or unpins its panel, moves the
button to another side, or takes it off the stripe; a button is also dragged to another place on any stripe, or
into the tree, which docks its panel there.

A panel is unpinned with `slide_panel()`, or Slide on its tab's menu, or by dropping its tab on a stripe: it leaves
the tree, keeping its place there, and when shown slides out over the dock beside its stripe in a sliding window,
which the user resizes from its inner edge.  It slides away on a click anywhere else in the application, other than
in a menu, a tooltip or while a modal window is up; on Escape that the widget with the keyboard focus does not use;
or from its stripe button.  `pin_panel()`, or Pin on its tab's menu or its button's menu, docks it where it was.

# The event dispatch {#design_dispatch}

The docks watch every event through `Fl::event_dispatch()`, to see clicks outside a sliding window and to follow the
keyboard focus, underlining the tab of the group that has it and telling the application with
`FL_REASON_GOT_FOCUS`.  `Fl_Dock::dispatch()` is installed as the first dock is made, in front of any dispatch the
application set before, which it calls; it is taken out as the last dock goes.  FLTK keeps a single dispatch
function, so an application that sets its own after making a dock must have it call the one it replaced, which it
reads with `Fl::event_dispatch()` first; otherwise the docks stop seeing events.

The sliding window is a subwindow of the dock's window, like an inset window, so it needs no window manager, stacks
above an OpenGL view, and works on Wayland.

# Whole-dock commands {#design_commands}

`hide_all()` hides every docked panel, leaving the center, and a second call puts them all back.  `maximize_panel()`
gives one panel's group the whole dock, the center included, until it is called again.  Both undo exactly, by
restoring the layout they saved first; changing the dock in between, or reading a layout, keeps the dock as it is then
and forgets what they would put back, and a layout written in between is the one they would put back.  `panel_menu()`
fills a menu with an item for each panel, checked while it is shown, which the dock keeps up to date.  `save_layout()`
and `load_layout()` keep named layouts in an `Fl_Preferences`.

# Saving and restoring {#design_saving}

A layout is text, one node per line and indented by depth, with a version line first:

```
fl_dock 1
dock
 split h 0.25 0.75
  tabs 0 files
  split v 0.7 0.3
   center
   tabs 1 props log
window 120 80 300 400
 tabs 0 notes
closed help
size 800 575
```

Stripes are saved as `stripe left name ... | name ...`, the buttons of each group in order, and unpinned panels as
`sliding name size`; a panel slid out is saved as closed and sliding.  `size` is the size of the dock's area, so a
layout read in a dock of another size is laid out at that size first and then fitted as the dock is resized, the
panels keeping their sizes.

`write_layout(buf, size)` writes it, returning the length it needs as `snprintf` does, and `read_layout(text)` reads
it; `save_layout()` and `load_layout()` keep it in an `Fl_Preferences`.  Names
are written with spaces, control characters and `%` as `%XX`.  Reading is all-or-nothing: the text is parsed and
checked first, and the dock is only changed if it is good, otherwise it is left as it was and -1 is returned.  The
version must be one this library reads, every share positive, every split two or more children, every name once,
the tree no deeper than `Fl_Dock::MAX_DEPTH`, 1000, and the center only in the dock and only if it has one.
Top-level sections a later version adds are skipped.  Every state the dock can reach writes a layout it reads back:
names are unique and not empty, `add_panel()` will not make the tree deeper than a layout may be, and a tree not yet
in normal form, as while a deleted widget is taken out, is written as its normal form.

Panels are found by name.  A name the dock does not know is passed to the panel factory, if the application gave one,
which must return a new panel of that name, and if none comes back its place is kept, so `place_panel()` puts the
panel there when the application creates it later.  Panels the text does not mention go back where they were, so a
panel new in this version of the application is never lost to an old saved layout.  Floating window positions are
hints, which Wayland may ignore.

# Notifications {#design_notifications}

As for other FLTK widgets, the dock's callback tells the application what the user did; changes made by calling the
dock's functions do not call it.  Each change the user makes sets the dock's `changed()` flag, and calls the
callback if `when()` has `FL_WHEN_CHANGED` or `FL_WHEN_RELEASE`, the default, with FLTK's callback reasons and the
panel given by `callback_panel()`: `FL_REASON_DRAGGED` when the user moves a panel, `FL_REASON_CLOSED` when they close
one, `FL_REASON_OPENED` when they show one, `FL_REASON_SELECTED` when they select a tab, `FL_REASON_GOT_FOCUS` when
a panel takes the keyboard focus, and `FL_REASON_CHANGED` when they drag a bar between two parts of the dock, with no
panel, resize an inset or sliding window, or maximize a panel.  Bars and window sizes call it as they move with
`FL_WHEN_CHANGED`, and when let go with `FL_WHEN_RELEASE`, so an application can save its layout on every change.

A panel may refuse to close: when the user closes it, from its tab or its window, the dock asks its virtual
`can_close()` first.  The tab strip's menu lists every panel in the group, so a tab that does not fit is still
reached.

# Not provided {#design_not_provided}

Compass-style drop indicators, theming beyond FLTK's box types and colors, animation of sliding windows, and undo.

*/
